\section{Locks}{

For a lock, \code{\link[=create_env]{create_env()}} picks the platform from the platforms that
the file lists: \code{platform} when it is given, else the native platform of
the backend, else an emulated one (see \code{platform} in \code{\link[=create_env]{create_env()}}).
When the file lists none of these, \code{create_env()} aborts with class
\code{condathis_create_env_platform_not_locked} (also
\code{condathis_create_env_platform_mismatch}) before anything runs.
A lock of \code{noarch} packages lists no platform, and installs on any
platform. For an \verb{@EXPLICIT} or mambajs file, micromamba takes the
platform of each package from its subdir, such as \code{noarch} or
\code{linux-64}, not from the \code{platform} header of the file.
\code{channels}, \code{additional_channels} and \code{channel_priority} cannot be given
with a lock: a lock names its packages by URL. They abort with class
\code{condathis_create_env_conflicting_args}. The deprecated \code{env_file}
argument of \code{\link[=create_env]{create_env()}} ignores them, as in 0.1.4. A lock that lists packages but
gives an empty environment aborts with class
\code{condathis_create_env_failed}.

When the backend reports the packages of a lock, as micromamba does for
\verb{@EXPLICIT}, conda-lock and mambajs files, an existing environment is
compared with them: the same names, versions and build strings, none
missing and none more, for the platform of the lock. Packages that pip
installed are left out. \code{create_env(overwrite = FALSE)} then keeps an
environment that has exactly those packages, and
\verb{validate_env(env_definition(<lock>), env_name = ...)} returns \code{TRUE}.
